  "name": "GTO to GEO",
  "description": "Two satellites go from a Cape Canaveral launch (28.5 deg) to geostationary orbit with the Change-orbit planner. 'Apogee kick' rides a 185 x 35,786 km transfer orbit and fires once at apogee, turning the whole 28.5 deg there, where it moves slowest. 'From parking' starts in a 185 km circular orbit: its first burn (the transfer injection) takes a little of the turn for almost nothing, the second does the rest at apogee. Apogee kick: one burn of 1837.6 m/s does it all (it fires 7.1 km from the target height), inclination 28.50 -> 0.00 deg; it reaches 0.003 deg, not 0: the burn is 0.003 deg off the equator (J2 moves the apsides off the nodes), so trim it later. From parking: dV1 2482.5 m/s (tilt -2.16 deg), dV2 1790.9 m/s (tilt -26.37 deg), total 4273.4 m/s, coast 5.26 h; it reaches 0.062 deg, not 0: the burn is 0.062 deg off the equator (J2 moves the apsides off the nodes), so trim it later; burn 2 takes 92% of the tilt: it fires at 1.60 km/s against 7.80, and turning a slower velocity costs less.",
